tgs:Interview.TGS_68_SimonMichaux a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simon Michaux: \"The Arcadian Blueprint\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct third conversation with his own real, close friend Simon Michaux, centered on Michaux's own real, recently-published paper 'A Resource Balanced Economy' — offered directly as a real, considered alternative to the popular 'circular economy' framing, which Michaux argues understates the real, structural resource and design changes actually required. The conversation explores what Nate calls 'the road to Arcadia,' a real, direct extension of Michaux's own established four-archetype framework for how different groups might respond to a post-growth transition."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-04-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-04-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/68-simon-michaux> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bb801wdRULM> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/TGS68SimonMichauxTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_19_SimonMichaux, tgs:Interview.TGS_49_SimonMichaux, tgs:Interview.TGS_53_WilliamRees ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Michaux directly argues genuine circularity is structurally undermined by how modern products are actually designed: complex alloys in something as ordinary as a smartphone combine dozens of real, specific elements in ways that make true material recovery genuinely difficult, meaning 'recyclable' framing often overstates what's actually achievable with current design practices. He's real and direct that a real, viable resource-balanced economy would likely require far more localized, shorter supply chains than today's real, global six-continent manufacturing networks — trading some efficiency and comparative advantage for genuine resilience and reduced fragility. A real, striking case study anchors his own optimism about ecological restoration: anthropologists dumped several hundred tons of discarded orange rind onto a barren, deforested patch of the Amazon, and within five to six years the area had genuinely reforested — offered as real, direct, physical proof that degraded land can recover once its underlying soil food web is deliberately rebuilt, not merely left alone. Closes with a real, warm, playful exchange distinct from the conversation's technical depth: Nate floats the idea of short, informal daily check-ins with Michaux based on whatever he'd sketched that morning, both acknowledging neither could realistically sustain it, before closing with real, direct mutual affection — 'thank you so much, my friend... you are a global treasure.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely encyclopedic/technical (comparative advantage, autarky, thorium reactors) rather than pointing to further individuals — William Rees and Art Berman are both directly cross-referenced and already built, not new finds."@en ;
    .


