tgs:Interview.TGS_70_JohnKitzhaber a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Kitzhaber: \"What Makes a Healthy Society?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts John Kitzhaber — former ER physician, three-term Governor of Oregon, and architect of the real Oregon Health Plan and Coordinated Care Organizations — for a direct critique of US healthcare as a real, structural case study in misallocated resources. US healthcare spending approaches 20% of GDP, roughly $12,900 per capita annually, while producing real, measurably worse outcomes than other developed nations — the highest infant mortality rate and lowest life expectancy among OECD peers."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-04-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-05-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnKitzhaber ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/70-john-kitzhaber> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z4cjl77rj78> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/TGS70JohnKitzhaberTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_154_JaneMuncke ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Kitzhaber directly traces the Oregon Health Plan's own real origin to a real, painful case: Coby Howard, a young Oregon boy whose Medicaid-funded bone marrow transplant was denied under the state's existing rationing rules, a real, public controversy that directly forced the state to confront resource allocation explicitly rather than implicitly. He's real and direct about US healthcare's structural misdirection: roughly 25 cents of every healthcare dollar produces no measurable positive outcome, while real social determinants — poverty, early-life investment, nutrition — account for the large majority of lifetime health status, with formal healthcare system contact itself responsible for only about 10%. A real, striking regulatory-capture statistic anchors his institutional critique: roughly a quarter of FDA employees go on to work for or consult with pharmaceutical companies, and about two-thirds of retired 2019 members of Congress who took jobs outside politics moved into lobbying — real, direct evidence of the same revolving-door dynamic driving healthcare policy broadly. Closes on real, direct personal reflection distinct from policy analysis: asked what he cares about most, Kitzhaber points directly to wanting to leave a healthy planet and society for his own 25-year-old son's generation, citing Thích Nhất Hạnh's real, direct metaphor of navigating by the North Star — not expecting to reach it, but using it to hold a consistent direction across a lifetime of uncertain progress. His own real, unhedged magic-wand answer is not policy but disposition: helping people momentarily set aside polarization to remember two things everyone genuinely shares — mortality, and the same underlying desire for meaning, value, and a fair opportunity to pursue their own path."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely statistical/institutional (CMS, OECD, FDA revolving-door reporting) rather than pointing to further individuals — no new deferred Persona candidates surfaced here."@en ;
    .


