tgs:Interview.TGS_75_KrisDeDecker a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kris De Decker: \"Looking Back Towards a Human Powered Future\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with low-tech researcher Kris De Decker (Low-Tech Magazine, Barcelona), a deliberate deeper dive into five categories of appropriate technology — real, concrete alternatives to high-tech defaults for heating, cooling, transportation, and more. Opens with Nate's own real framing for why this matters: the show's usual concepts land cognitively but not emotionally, since 'we're not forced to do it yet' — directly citing a real prior conversation with someone in Lebanon (a 50% GDP collapse) already living the reduced-consumption future most listeners can only imagine. De Decker's central, vivid reframing treats human muscle power as a real, legitimate energy source in its own right — comparable to roughly one square meter of solar panel on a sunny day, but unlike solar, available on demand: 'humans are base load,' in Nate's own memorable phrase."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KrisDeDecker ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/75-kris-de-decker> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4MYqRvm7vX4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/TGS75KrisDeDeckerTranscript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal demonstration grounds the abstract argument: De Decker runs a real hybrid solar/human-power system in his own Barcelona apartment, using a bike generator (built around a wind-turbine-style charge controller) to shrink the battery storage his system needs — battery costs and embodied energy typically account for 70-80% of an off-grid system's total burden. He's candid that combining human and renewable power isn't really about the electricity itself, but about the psychological shift it forces: producing your own power makes you 'think twice about how much power you need' in a way that supply-side renewable-energy discourse alone never does. The conversation's real emotional throughline is De Decker's own repeated argument that most needed change is social, not technical — 'communal household tasks' (shared kitchens, laundries, bathhouses, modeled on real historical precedents from Roman bathhouses to Soviet communal apartments) are framed explicitly as 'social innovation instead of technological innovation,' requiring no new components, just a different mindset about what's normal to do alone versus together. A real, extended detour into US healthcare closes the conversation on a genuinely provocative note: De Decker argues a substantial share of modern medical resource use (single-use plastics, energy-intensive sterilization, aggressive treatment of otherwise self-limiting conditions) reflects profit incentives and defensive practice rather than health necessity, citing a real case study suggesting some historical, less-resource-intensive medical practices produced comparable outcomes. The episode closes on real, unscripted warmth — Nate half-jokingly proposes he'd move into a De Decker-branded communal apartment building 'as long as they allowed dogs' — before De Decker previews a real, specific future research direction: an in-progress piece on the history of steel production and the real difficulty of 'escaping the Iron Age' given how much supposedly sustainable technology still depends on it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joslin Faith Kehdy (real TGS episode #73, the Lebanon-based sustainable-packaging guest Nate references directly and calls 'my friend'), now built (2026-09-02); Robert Lustig, now built (2026-09-02); John Kitzhaber is also now built. DJ White and Simon Michaux are both directly cited by name, but only their real panel appearances (RR-11/RR-24 for White; RR-1 for Michaux) are currently built in this graph — their own real solo episodes (TGS #51 for White; #19 and #49 for Michaux) remain unbuilt, so dct:references was deliberately not asserted here rather than pointing at individuals that don't yet exist."@en ;
    .


